//
// A struct literal that OMITS a field takes that field's zero
// silently — no warning, both backends — and there is no way to tell
// the omission from an author who wrote the zero on purpose.
//
// The ask is a DIAGNOSTIC, not a semantics change, and the fix it
// would point at already exists: loft supports a declared field
// default (`palette_pick: integer = -1`), and the third case below
// shows it working.  Nothing points you at it, so a project that does
// not know the feature exists builds a second field instead — which is
// what dryopea did.
//
// ── Why the zero is the dangerous value ─────────────────────────
//
// It is dangerous exactly when zero is a MEANINGFUL value of the
// field's domain.  dryopea's `EditorInput` (src/editor_step.loft) is
// one frame of player intent, built field-by-field by callers that do
// not know what was added to the struct last week.  Its palette pick
// wants a "nothing selected" sentinel of -1 — and an omitted field
// makes that 0, which is palette entry 0, which is SEA, which erases
// the hex under the cursor.
//
// So dryopea carries a two-field workaround — a boolean
// `in_select_palette` beside the `in_palette_index` — for the sole
// purpose of making "no action" the zero of every field.  A declared
// default of -1 would have done it in one field, and a warning on the
// partial literal is what would have found the feature.
//
// Expected output:
//     no default, field omitted  : 0        <- silent; -1 was meant
//     no default, field written  : -1
//     declared default, omitted  : -1       <- the feature that fixes it

struct NoDefault {
    hover:        integer,
    palette_pick: integer,
}

struct WithDefault {
    hover:        integer,
    palette_pick: integer = -1,
}

fn main() {
    // 1. The field is omitted.  It reads 0, and 0 selects a palette
    //    entry — the author meant "nothing selected".  No diagnostic
    //    of any kind, on either backend.
    a = NoDefault { hover: 3 };
    println("no default, field omitted  : {a.palette_pick}");

    // 2. The same struct with the sentinel written out — the only
    //    version that means what the author intended, and nothing
    //    tells them the one above differs.
    b = NoDefault { hover: 3, palette_pick: -1 };
    println("no default, field written  : {b.palette_pick}");

    // 3. A DECLARED default makes the omission safe.  This is the fix
    //    a warning on case 1 would lead the author to.
    c = WithDefault { hover: 3 };
    println("declared default, omitted  : {c.palette_pick}");
}
